WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.188
"Onions and Tomatoes
Put to the Test for Osteoporosis

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.180
Feeding rats
dozens of different foods,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.460
the fruit found to preserve
their bones the best

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.660
was the prune,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.760
and the leading vegetable
was the onion.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.740
I've talked already
about prunes.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.180
What about onions?

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.160
The country with apparently

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.840
the highest per capita
consumption of onions

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.920
in the world is Turkey,

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.320
which also has
one of the lowest rates

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.820
of osteoporotic bone fractures.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.220
Turkey may have
four times greater

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.500
daily per capita onion intake
than the United States

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.080
and four times lower hip
fracture probabilities.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.720
Is that just
a coincidence, though?

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.000
The problem with
trying to correlate

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.560
country-by-country comparisons

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.900
is that you don't know if the
people within those countries

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.280
who are actually
eating those onions

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.260
are the ones who are actually
avoiding fractures.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.380
But in 2017, a prospective
study was published

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.490
in which the fruit
and vegetable intake

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.880
of about 1,500 older women was
followed for nearly 15 years,

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.160
and of all of
the classes of vegetables,

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.080
the intake of
allium family veggies

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.700
such as onions,
leeks, and garlic

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.380
were the ones most associated

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.480
with lower risk
of bone fractures.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.720
Based on a study of non-Hispanic
white women 50 years and older,

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.760
those who ate onions
on a daily basis

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.340
had an overall bone density
5 percent greater

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.720
than those who rarely ate them.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.760
That may not sound like a lot

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.620
but could potentially
translate into reducing

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.900
the risk of hip fracture
by more than 20 percent.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.600
Why onions?

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.520
Maybe it's the quercetin.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.920
Onions are one of the
most concentrated sources

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:29.940
of this phytonutrient,

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.640
which can stimulate the activity
of our bone-building cells,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:33.540
at least in a petri dish,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.220
and is also a potent inhibitor

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.200
of the formation
of new bone-eating cells.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.500
Or could it be
the fructan-fiber

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.940
prebiotics in onions?

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.540
Experimentally infused
into the rectum,

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.340
the short-chain fatty acids

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.560
created by our
fiber-eating gut flora

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.900
have been shown
to stimulate calcium absorption,

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.820
so much so that adolescents

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.860
randomized to the type
of fiber found in onions--

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.440
about an onion a day's worth
for a year--

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.500
significantly increased
their bone mineral density

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.880
over the placebo group.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.900
In the rodent bone
preservation study,

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.240
a number of spices beat out
prunes and onions,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.180
with the top dog being
another allium, garlic.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.400
And, in the prospective
human study,

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.260
cruciferous vegetables
like broccoli

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.840
came in at a close second
to the allium family.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.780
Although there are
some petri dish data

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.700
supporting the potential
bone benefits

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.800
of the sulforaphane
in cruciferous veggies,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.140
inhibiting the formation
of bone-eating cells,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.000
and it appears to protect
bone health in mice,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.220
the reason
I'm singling out onions

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.320
is that it's one
of only two vegetables

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.800
that have actually been put
to the test in clinical trials.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.940
But how are you going
to come up with a placebo onion

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.200
for the control group?

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.200
That's why a group of
innovative Chinese researchers

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.520
gave people onion juice
versus a fake onion juice.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.120
I don't know which sounds worse,
but anything for science!

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.820
And, those randomized
to the real onion group

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:57.900
experienced an improvement

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.640
in a marker of bone loss
over the placebo,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.980
but the study
didn't last long enough

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.160
to see if this translated
into tangible bone benefits.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.540
But a clinical trial

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.300
on the other vegetable
put to the test did.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.560
The tomato story starts out
like the onion story.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.480
There's epidemiological support.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.940
In the Framingham
Osteoporosis Study,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.700
higher intakes of lycopene,
the red pigment in tomatoes,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.740
were associated with
protection against bone loss

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.540
in older men and women
over a period of four years,

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.640
as well as protection
against hip fractures

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.660
over 17 years.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:30.180
Perhaps this helps explain
why studies show

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.420
that increased adherence to
a more Mediterranean-style diet

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.060
is associated with about
20 percent fewer hip fractures.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.220
Then there's
laboratory evidence.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:41.040
Lycopene inhibits bone loss
in a petri dish

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.260
and preserves bone mass in rats.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.080
However, so does
green tomato extract,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.660
which is richer in compounds
such as tomatine

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:49.800
rather than lycopene,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.560
so maybe there are multiple
protective factors in tomatoes.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.800
Anyway, let's feed people
some tomato products

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.043
and see what happens!

00:03:57.043 --> 00:03:59.040
Postmenopausal women
randomized to lycopene

00:03:59.040 --> 00:03:59.940
in the form of about

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.340
a cup and a third of
regular tomato juice a day

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:03.960
experienced
a significant reduction

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.300
in a marker of bone loss
by month two,

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.880
and the opposite was
found after just a month

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.280
of restricting
lycopene consumption,

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.800
so, no tomatoes, watermelon,
or other red fruits

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:14.580
like pink grapefruit.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.420
This suggests that just regular
dietary intakes are protective.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.600
But does this translate
into retaining significantly

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:22.620
more bone over time?

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:25.440
Postmenopausal women given
about two-thirds of a cup

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.120
of tomato sauce a day
for three months

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.580
suffered significantly
less bone loss

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.980
than those in an age-matched
group who didn't,

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.960
though it does not appear

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.420
that the study subjects
were assigned randomly,

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:38.040
which could bias the results.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:42.300
So, should we go out of our way
to include these specific fruits

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.260
and vegetables in our diet?

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.620
Normally, we're just left
with a "can't-hurt" shrug,

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.960
but a group
of New Zealand researchers

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.700
put together
a randomized controlled trial

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:51.420
to find out.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.600
They developed
the Scarborough Fair Diet,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.760
named for the presence
of presumptive

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.420
bone-protecting herbs parsley,
sage, rosemary, and thyme

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.540
from the song popularized
by Simon &amp; Garfunkel.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.300
The diet included prunes,
onions, and tomatoes.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.060
They compared that
to a diet similarly packed

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:10.680
with nine or more servings
of fruits and vegetables,

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.200
but ones that were
not suspected as having

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.240
particularly
skeleton-saving properties.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:18.540
Markers of bone turnover were
measured after three months,

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.480
and the specially concocted
diet of bone-preserving produce

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.280
did no significantly better

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.980
than the diet packed with
non-bone preserving produce,

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.680
or a diet with just six servings
of fruits and veggies a day,

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:30.780
suggesting that
the focus should just be on

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.080
stuffing your face with fruits
and vegetables of any stripe.

